<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shamshi-Adad I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1808–1776 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> I was an Amorite ruler who reigned c. 1808–1776 BCE and built a kingdom across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, including the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>. He is regarded as an important figure in early Assyrian history. His kingdom collapsed after his death, opening the way for the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i.jpg" alt="Shamshi-Adad I" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adelheid Otto · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shamshi-Adad_I.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Shamshi-Adad I was an Amorite ruler of Ekallatum who became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and conquered lands across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, creating what is called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a>. He inherited the throne of Ekallatum from Ila-kabkabu, whom the later Assyrian King List names as his father. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> attacked Ekallatum, Shamshi-Adad fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and stayed in exile until Naram-Sin died.</p><p>He then retook Ekallatum, overthrew King Erishum II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, emerging as the first Amorite king of Assyria. Later Assyrian tradition regarded him as an Amorite, though earlier archaeologists wrongly took him for a native Assyrian. At Assur he held the title Governor of Assur and rebuilt the temple of the god Assur, whose bricks carry his name as builder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>He took over the long-abandoned town of Shekhna, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-leilan">Tell Leilan</a>, and renamed it Šubat-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a>, &quot;the residence of the god Enlil&quot;, making it his capital. A main target of his expansion was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, which controlled the caravan route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahdun-lim">Yahdun-Lim</a> was assassinated by his own servants, possibly on Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>'s orders, and the heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> fled to Yamhad. Shamshi-Adad occupied Mari and placed his sons in charge: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishme-dagan-i">Ishme-Dagan I</a> at Ekallatum and Yasmah-Adad at Mari. He proclaimed himself &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-universe">King of the Universe</a>&quot;, a title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> had used.</p><p>His relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> alternated between alliance and war. Around 1781 BCE he joined King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadusha">Dadusha</a> in a campaign between the two Zab rivers, then turned on Dadusha and attacked cities including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-harmal">Shaduppum</a>, Nerebtum and Andarig. His campaigns were carefully planned, and his army used encircling ramparts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battering-ram">battering rams</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His letters to his sons show a ruler who controlled state business closely. He scolded Yasmah-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>: &quot;Are you a child, not a man, have you no beard on your chin?&quot; He also arranged for Yasmah-Adad to marry Beltum, daughter of Ishi-Addu of Qatna, and insisted that she be kept in a leading position in the palace.</p><p>The kingdom lacked cohesion, and after his death around 1776 BCE Eshnunna captured cities around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> while old rivals moved against his sons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> expelled Yasmah-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishme-dagan-i">Ishme-Dagan I</a> the rest was lost to a coalition of Mari, Andarig and Eshnunna and then to the Amorite ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shamshi-Adad I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Territorial ambition first appeared under the Amorite conqueror <b>Shamshi-Adad I</b> (r. c. 1808–1776 BCE), who seized Assur, styled himself &quot;King of the Universe&quot; and built a kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia stretching from the Zagros foothills to the Euphrates and Mari.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They completed the walls, dug canals, won modest victories and survived in a world dominated by Elam to the east, the rival cities of Isin and Larsa to the south, and the Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia under <b>Shamshi-Adad I</b> to the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Soon after his death Mari fell to <b>Shamshi-Adad</b>, ruler of the Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia, who placed his son Yasmah-Addu on the throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Shubat-Enlil, at Tell Leilan in the Khabur plains, was the old city of Shehna, renamed by <b>Shamshi-Adad</b> as the capital of his Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/state/apum">Apum</a></li><li><blockquote>Chagar Bazar, excavated by Max Mallowan in the 1930s, served as a provincial centre under <b>Shamshi-Adad</b>'s Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia in the early 18th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/ashnakkum">Ashnakkum</a></li><li><blockquote>It emerged as an independent kingdom after the death of <b>Shamshi-Adad</b> and the break-up of the Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia in the 1770s BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/ilansura">Ilansura</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-leilan">Tell Leilan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.96,41.51&amp;year=-1792" aria-label="Tell Leilan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5591 832 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Shamshi-Adad I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6041 1102h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6041 1102h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.96,41.51&amp;year=-1792">What was here in 1792 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
